# =============================================================================
# experiment/contactangle/wedgeres150_T-20_theta120.opts
#
# WEDGE, vapour + thermal reservoirs. The companion to wedge150_*, which is
# sealed; run both to separate what the boundary condition does from what the
# contact angle does.
#
# These BCs are byte-identical to the 2026-08-07 wedge_bc campaign's rhov_eq_eq
# case: T pinned at -20 C on every face, vapour Dirichlet at saturation on the
# x-faces only, no imposed gradient. So the contact angle is the only thing that
# differs from that campaign, and the only driver within these runs.
#
# Expect the angle to come out slightly OFF Young here, and that is the point of
# running the pair. A reservoir keeps the interface exchanging mass, so it never
# reaches steady state; its meniscus carries a Gibbs-Thomson supersaturation and
# a moving interface satisfies sigma = d0*chi + beta*v_n rather than d0*chi
# alone. In the flat channel that cost -1.019 deg at theta=60 against +0.004
# sealed. What the reservoir buys in exchange is NET growth or shrinkage, which
# a sealed box cannot show at all -- vapour there holds ~1e-6 of the water.
#
# t_final = 150 days ~ 6 tau at the wedge-scale channel's tau = 24-34 d. Longer
# than the 90-day runs because the point here is the plateau, not the rate, and
# the flat-90 IC makes the approach cheaper anyway.
# =============================================================================
-t_final 1.3e7

-temp -20.0
-humidity 1.00
-grad_temp0 0.0,0.0,0.0

-beta_sub0 3.9704e6                     # K&P beta0 at -20 C (alpha_c = 2e-3)
-d0_sub0 1.0166e-9                      # physical capillary length at -20 C

# --- the substrate ---
# gamma_ia = 0.109 (the solver default), gamma_as - gamma_is = 0.0545
#   cos(theta) = -0.0545/0.109 = -0.5000  ->  theta = 120 deg
-gamma_as 0.300
-gamma_is 0.3545000

# --- reservoirs: T on every face, vapour on the x-faces only ---
# Pinning vapour on the y walls would feed ice directly at its contact line and
# corrupt the angle; pinning T there is harmless, regolith being a thermal bath.
-flag_BC_Tfix 1
-flag_BC_rhovfix 1
-rhovfix_axis 0
-rhovfix_lo 1.000000
-rhovfix_hi 1.000000

-dtCFL 1
-dtCFL_dphimax 0.2
-outp 0
-n_out 60
